{#-
  Setting HUE alone, or SATURATION alone.

  Home Assistant has no hue-only or saturation-only service — `hs_color` takes
  the pair together. webCoRE could change one and leave the other, so writing a
  constant for the other half silently recolours the light: a soft pastel told
  "set hue to 120" would come back fully saturated.

  So the half that was NOT asked for is read back from the light itself, at HA's
  own runtime. That is the behaviour webcore_vocab.json already records for
  setHue and setSaturation ("compile with template reading current hs_color").

  ONE ENTITY PER CALL. A single service call sends the same data to every target,
  and each light has its own current colour — so the compiler emits one of these
  per light rather than one call for all of them.

  The fallback matters: a light that is OFF, or that has never been given a
  colour, reports no hs_color at all. Full saturation / zero hue is the same
  assumption webCoRE's own behaviour implies, and it is applied only when there
  is genuinely nothing to preserve.

  This is HOME ASSISTANT TEMPLATE SYNTAX — state_attr, the hs_color attribute,
  the list shape — so it lives here rather than in Python. If HA changes any of
  it, this is where a user fixes it (COMPILER_SPEC "Jinja2 everywhere").

  SINGLE quotes only: the automation template wraps this in double quotes.

  Input:
    entity   the light this call targets
    value    the half the piston asked for
    keep     which half to preserve — 'saturation' when setting hue, 'hue' when
             setting saturation
-#}
{%- if keep == 'saturation' -%}
{{ '{{' }} [{{ value }}, (state_attr('{{ entity }}', 'hs_color') or [0, 100])[1]] {{ '}}' }}
{%- else -%}
{{ '{{' }} [(state_attr('{{ entity }}', 'hs_color') or [0, 100])[0], {{ value }}] {{ '}}' }}
{%- endif -%}
